in my life, you know?

00:00:09.616 --> 00:00:11.275
"Since You've Been Gone," really helped me

00:00:11.275 --> 00:00:12.807
when my dad left the house-

00:00:12.807 --> 00:00:13.640
- Oh wow.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.953
(Kelly laughing)
- But you know, now it's my-

00:00:15.953 --> 00:00:17.783
- He's like, "Thank you."

00:00:17.783 --> 00:00:19.888
- But now it's, "My Life
Would Suck Without You."

00:00:19.888 --> 00:00:21.298
- Yeah. I like that.

00:00:21.298 --> 00:00:22.757
Thank you, that was very nice.

00:00:22.757 --> 00:00:23.590
- Thank you so much.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:25.110
- I'm a big fan of what y'all are doing.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.539
I think this is the coolest thing ever.

00:00:26.539 --> 00:00:28.201
And I think a lot of families,

00:00:28.201 --> 00:00:30.262
especially, I come from
a family where I actually

00:00:30.262 --> 00:00:31.095
was estranged.

00:00:31.095 --> 00:00:33.434
I had an estranged father and
it didn't end up working out.

00:00:33.434 --> 00:00:37.229
And what I think is beautiful
is that it was hard for y'all,

00:00:37.229 --> 00:00:40.158
but now you have this really amazing show

00:00:40.158 --> 00:00:42.699
that's like, really cool
and it shows hope, I think,

00:00:42.699 --> 00:00:43.556
for a lot of people.

00:00:43.556 --> 00:00:45.895
'Cause some of us try.
- Yes, absolutely.

00:00:45.895 --> 00:00:48.186
- Some of us try, you know,
and you try and you try,

00:00:48.186 --> 00:00:49.199
and it just doesn't work.

00:00:49.199 --> 00:00:50.706
And so it's a really beautiful thing.

00:00:50.706 --> 00:00:51.862
So I'm excited to talk about it.

00:00:51.862 --> 00:00:52.907
But before we do that,

00:00:52.907 --> 00:00:56.492
I have to say it's the 20th
anniversary of your sitcom,

00:00:56.492 --> 00:00:57.768
"George Lopez."
- Already!

00:00:57.768 --> 00:00:58.859
- Yes!
- Oh my goodness!

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:01.074
(audience applauding)

00:01:01.074 --> 00:01:01.907
- [Kelly] It's so funny.

00:01:01.907 --> 00:01:03.913
So what's your favorite
memory from that time?

00:01:03.913 --> 00:01:06.147
- Well, you know, it's very tough, Kelly,

00:01:06.147 --> 00:01:08.872
to be 42 in the morning and 61 at night.

00:01:08.872 --> 00:01:10.170
(Kelly laughing)

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:13.348
But my favorite memory,
I think, is, you know,

00:01:13.348 --> 00:01:15.452
the odds of of having a television show,

00:01:15.452 --> 00:01:18.346
and having it be successful,
are like a million to one.

00:01:18.346 --> 00:01:21.523
But then to see your young
daughter be around the set,

00:01:21.523 --> 00:01:23.195
five years old, seven years old.

00:01:23.195 --> 00:01:25.037
Couple of times she was on the show.

00:01:25.037 --> 00:01:25.870
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:27.425
- And then to go back,

00:01:27.425 --> 00:01:32.161
and then see her now as
this incredibly intelligent

00:01:32.161 --> 00:01:34.328
and forgiving young Gen Z-

00:01:35.337 --> 00:01:36.170
- Yeah.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:39.384
- Is therapy well spent, Kelly.

00:01:39.384 --> 00:01:41.862
- Yeah. So wait, what was
that experience for you

00:01:41.862 --> 00:01:42.774
at five and seven?

00:01:42.774 --> 00:01:44.415
I'm curious 'cause my
kids have been around set

00:01:44.415 --> 00:01:45.646
and they're six and eight right now.

00:01:45.646 --> 00:01:46.604
- Yeah.
- Was that cool

00:01:46.604 --> 00:01:47.687
to be on set?
- I mean it was amazing

00:01:47.687 --> 00:01:49.403
'cause, you know, you always hear,

00:01:49.403 --> 00:01:52.339
especially from school,
especially, like, you know,

00:01:52.339 --> 00:01:54.706
"Oh. your dad's on TV. Your dad's on TV."

00:01:54.706 --> 00:01:55.539
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:55.539 --> 00:01:56.849
- But, you know, going and being able

00:01:56.849 --> 00:01:59.516
to see how people work together,

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:02.415
just seeing my dad being
able to do what he does

00:02:02.415 --> 00:02:05.075
and me laughing along with him,

00:02:05.075 --> 00:02:06.377
and you, know, I used to go

00:02:06.377 --> 00:02:09.166
and actually make craft services around.

00:02:09.166 --> 00:02:10.987
I used to actually make
little quesadillas.

00:02:10.987 --> 00:02:12.623
- That's my kid's favorite.
- And I used to walk around

00:02:12.623 --> 00:02:14.132
and give them to the whole stage-

00:02:14.132 --> 00:02:14.992
- Oh, you're a polite.

00:02:14.992 --> 00:02:16.709
My kids just take from
craft craft services.

00:02:16.709 --> 00:02:18.380
- I know!
- You're like serving people.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:19.964
My kids are like, "Where are the M&amp;Ms?"

00:02:19.964 --> 00:02:20.927
(George laughing)

00:02:20.927 --> 00:02:22.253
- Yeah, that's amazing.
- You know, but it's like,

00:02:22.253 --> 00:02:24.121
you know, formative of
being the backstage,

00:02:24.121 --> 00:02:25.828
seeing how things work, you get to see,

00:02:25.828 --> 00:02:27.489
and also how hard your parent works.

00:02:27.489 --> 00:02:30.173
You kind of get to have that
work ethic instilled in you

00:02:30.173 --> 00:02:32.238
when you kind of, at least it was for me.

00:02:32.238 --> 00:02:33.573
- So nobody tells you in the moment,

00:02:33.573 --> 00:02:35.377
they wait 14 years and they say,

00:02:35.377 --> 00:02:37.190
"You worked so hard. Thank you so much".

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.392
- And look, I literally
was about to say that.

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:40.897
I'm like, that's literally why I do.

00:02:40.897 --> 00:02:42.229
I bring my kids sometimes
to work because I'm like,

00:02:42.229 --> 00:02:44.505
I don't want you to think
I'm just like, leaving.

00:02:44.505 --> 00:02:45.933
I'm the breadwinner.

00:02:45.933 --> 00:02:47.148
You know, I'm the one making money for us.

00:02:47.148 --> 00:02:48.198
I'm the one like holding it down

00:02:48.198 --> 00:02:49.941
and it's nice for them to get to see.

00:02:49.941 --> 00:02:51.097
I think they appreciate it.

00:02:51.097 --> 00:02:52.647
They're like, "Oh, we see what you do."

00:02:52.647 --> 00:02:55.615
And I think that is actually
cool 'cause they don't get it.

00:02:55.615 --> 00:02:57.494
They just think, "Why is
everybody else mom get to come

00:02:57.494 --> 00:02:59.334
to science lab and you don't?"

00:02:59.334 --> 00:03:01.320
And you're like, I try.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:02.833
But do you remember the first time

00:03:02.833 --> 00:03:04.166
that you made your dad laugh?

00:03:04.166 --> 00:03:05.816
Did that feel like success?

00:03:05.816 --> 00:03:08.267
- Yes. You know, I used to
love "Singing in the Rain."

00:03:08.267 --> 00:03:09.653
It's still one of my favorite movies

00:03:09.653 --> 00:03:11.424
and there's a song that
says "Make Him Laugh."

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:13.988
And there's a whole sequence
where there's a dummy,

00:03:13.988 --> 00:03:16.364
and he's playing with the
dummy and throwing it around,

00:03:16.364 --> 00:03:18.737
and I was like, oh my God.

00:03:18.737 --> 00:03:20.072
That was one of the
first things I really saw

00:03:20.072 --> 00:03:23.093
that I was like, you can
be crazy, and slap stick.

00:03:23.093 --> 00:03:23.926
and funny.

00:03:23.926 --> 00:03:26.314
And so I remember my, I
had my mom go to Universal

00:03:26.314 --> 00:03:29.128
to like one of those little
shops and get me a little doll.

00:03:29.128 --> 00:03:30.702
- Yeah.
- And I did it on the couch

00:03:30.702 --> 00:03:31.860
in the middle of the living room.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:32.826
- You reenacted it?

00:03:32.826 --> 00:03:34.992
- Acted it from the beginning to end.

00:03:34.992 --> 00:03:37.852
And, it was, you know,
being able to perform.

00:03:37.852 --> 00:03:39.633
I think being in a house of performers

00:03:39.633 --> 00:03:41.460
and people being in the industry.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.429
You know, my parents
have always been like,

00:03:43.429 --> 00:03:44.821
"Go for your dream."

00:03:44.821 --> 00:03:46.361
Just try to be the best comedian

00:03:46.361 --> 00:03:47.484
that you can be.
- Yeah.

00:03:47.484 --> 00:03:49.397
Speaking of children, you
wrote a children's book, right?

00:03:49.397 --> 00:03:50.893
- I did write a children's book, yes.

00:03:50.893 --> 00:03:53.454
It's called "ChupaCarter"

00:03:53.454 --> 00:03:54.986
- [Kelly] Chupacabra, like the, okay.

00:03:54.986 --> 00:03:59.387
- And it's about a young boy
who befriends a chupacabra

00:03:59.387 --> 00:04:02.253
and he grows up, his grandparents,

00:04:02.253 --> 00:04:03.835
like myself, my grandparents raised me,

00:04:03.835 --> 00:04:04.939
and I would always spend
a lot of time, Kelly,

00:04:04.939 --> 00:04:06.907
looking at, at the moon at night,

00:04:06.907 --> 00:04:08.611
and I would wonder, looking at the moon,

00:04:08.611 --> 00:04:11.242
where all the people would be
that were gonna be in my life

00:04:11.242 --> 00:04:12.419
when I was an adult.

00:04:12.419 --> 00:04:14.122
So it's one of those kind of metaphysical,

00:04:14.122 --> 00:04:15.574
but it's also very Latino.

00:04:15.574 --> 00:04:19.855
You know, all of our stories
are, you know, horror in genre.

00:04:19.855 --> 00:04:22.532
Even kids stories, you know, La Llorona.

00:04:22.532 --> 00:04:25.536
- They are!
- La Llorona and her kids.

00:04:25.536 --> 00:04:27.471
And then, I was telling this white woman

00:04:27.471 --> 00:04:29.343
and she was listening to me going,

00:04:29.343 --> 00:04:31.863
"Well I hope you called Child
Protective Services because

00:04:31.863 --> 00:04:33.204
(Kelly laughing)

00:04:33.204 --> 00:04:35.216
if you didn't, you're
as guilty as your aunt."

00:04:35.216 --> 00:04:38.180
(Kelly laughing)

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:39.013
It was a story,
- No, it was fine.

00:04:39.013 --> 00:04:39.846
- It was a story.

00:04:39.846 --> 00:04:40.829
- It was a story.

00:04:40.829 --> 00:04:42.799
So wait, can you explain the chubacabra,

00:04:42.799 --> 00:04:43.992
for those who don't know?

00:04:43.992 --> 00:04:46.365
- A chubacabra is what would be,

00:04:46.365 --> 00:04:50.720
it's a type of animal
that could be a human-

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:54.545
- It's like wolf hybrid.
- It's wolf hybrid of a goat.

00:04:54.545 --> 00:04:56.365
Maybe it eats goats.

00:04:56.365 --> 00:04:58.782
No one's ever really seen it.

00:05:00.378 --> 00:05:03.520
It's in South America,
but then it's in Mexico,

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:04.945
and it's out here in Los Angeles.

00:05:04.945 --> 00:05:05.778
- [Mayan] It's like a Big Foot figure.

00:05:05.778 --> 00:05:06.611
- Like a Big Foot.
- Yes.

00:05:06.611 --> 00:05:07.981
- I was gonna say, is this
like a Big Foot thing?

00:05:07.981 --> 00:05:08.930
No one's seen it?
- Yeah.

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:12.430
- It's like a Big Foot in
a smaller, Latino shape.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:14.198
(everyone laughing)

00:05:14.198 --> 00:05:17.995
Or like a Big Foot in Halloween
candy, the little fun size.

00:05:17.995 --> 00:05:21.191
You're not afraid, and then
all your bloods drain from you,

00:05:21.191 --> 00:05:22.024
then you're afraid.

00:05:22.024 --> 00:05:25.783
And so it's about befriending somebody-

00:05:25.783 --> 00:05:27.198
- Evil.
- And, you know,

00:05:27.198 --> 00:05:29.428
it's like E.T, you know,
I mean just not even

00:05:29.428 --> 00:05:30.640
in these immigration,

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:32.729
everybody wants to go home at some point.

00:05:32.729 --> 00:05:34.648
So it's a bit of that.

00:05:34.648 --> 00:05:36.828
They take a journey and
they find each other.

00:05:36.828 --> 00:05:39.277
And then from not having a family,

00:05:39.277 --> 00:05:42.777
the ChupaCarter family help my little self

00:05:43.898 --> 00:05:45.444
and put a family together.

00:05:45.444 --> 00:05:46.532
- Oh, that's great.
- Yeah.

00:05:46.532 --> 00:05:47.542
(audience applauding)

00:05:47.542 --> 00:05:48.989
- So the news show, "Lopez Vs. Lopez"

00:05:48.989 --> 00:05:50.703
is based on your lives, right?

00:05:50.703 --> 00:05:52.750
- Yeah. It's loosely
based on our real life.

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:54.250
- [Kelly] Is that hard?

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:55.690
- You know what, I'm not
gonna say it's loosely.

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:57.590
I'm the one that did all that was wrong.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:05:58.966
It's not a loosely based.

00:05:58.966 --> 00:06:02.667
- It's like, I know, but I'm
just saying, is that hard?

00:06:02.667 --> 00:06:03.905
I don't know. It's hard for me.

00:06:03.905 --> 00:06:06.250
Sometimes I write a lot about
my real life in songs, right.

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:08.170
It's hard for me to like, rehash that.

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:09.003
- Yeah.
- Is that hard?

00:06:09.003 --> 00:06:11.294
- I have to say there are
times where it is challenging,

00:06:11.294 --> 00:06:13.402
but where I like to think is that,

00:06:13.402 --> 00:06:15.496
this is such an opportunity to heal

00:06:15.496 --> 00:06:18.495
and I always wanted my
pain to mean something and-

00:06:18.495 --> 00:06:19.328
- Yeah.
- You know what,

00:06:19.328 --> 00:06:22.686
as you have done, like to reach
out and carry much as people

00:06:22.686 --> 00:06:24.507
and know that healing isn't linear

00:06:24.507 --> 00:06:25.651
but you're not perfect and-

00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:26.484
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:26.484 --> 00:06:27.901
- You know, just having empathy, you know,

00:06:27.901 --> 00:06:29.533
as you get older you start
to see your parents as people

00:06:29.533 --> 00:06:30.366
and-
- Yeah.

00:06:30.366 --> 00:06:32.170
- You know, maybe you
understand some things.

00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:33.807
You don't have to forgive
them, but you can understand,

00:06:33.807 --> 00:06:34.640
and-
- Yeah.

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.164
- You know, going backwards and healing

00:06:36.164 --> 00:06:38.424
but also rebuilding something
stronger for the future.

00:06:38.424 --> 00:06:39.678
- [Kelly] You're very healthy.

00:06:39.678 --> 00:06:40.914
- I'm very therapized.
- Wow.

00:06:40.914 --> 00:06:41.801
I was like-
- Everyone's like,

00:06:41.801 --> 00:06:42.634
"Where have you been?"

00:06:42.634 --> 00:06:43.712
- That's amazing!
- I'm like, "Therapy."

00:06:43.712 --> 00:06:44.795
- [Kelly] I was like, wow.

00:06:44.795 --> 00:06:45.628
(audience applauding)

00:06:45.628 --> 00:06:49.389
- I would say that that
message is probably conveyed

00:06:49.389 --> 00:06:51.123
much better in song.

00:06:51.123 --> 00:06:52.800
It sounds better somebody saying

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.571
♪ You were never there for me. ♪

00:06:55.571 --> 00:06:57.766
As opposed to, "You were
never there for me!"

00:06:57.766 --> 00:06:58.927
- Yeah, yeah.

00:06:58.927 --> 00:07:02.061
If you lighten it with a
nice melancholy melody.

00:07:02.061 --> 00:07:03.531
- Yes, yes. Exactly.

00:07:03.531 --> 00:07:06.325
- But it's good though, that
y'all have that communicative,

00:07:06.325 --> 00:07:07.507
I wish I would've had that.

00:07:07.507 --> 00:07:09.365
Like, my father passed
away, so we never got that

00:07:09.365 --> 00:07:10.353
and I tried so hard.

00:07:10.353 --> 00:07:12.412
A lot of people really
want that, and desire that,

00:07:12.412 --> 00:07:13.245
and don't get that.
- Right.

00:07:13.245 --> 00:07:15.496
- So it's really remarkable and awesome.

00:07:15.496 --> 00:07:16.934
- We believe, you know, the first show

00:07:16.934 --> 00:07:18.315
had a different slant.

00:07:18.315 --> 00:07:19.850
You know, this is fathers and daughters,

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:22.162
and that relationship is worth, you know,

00:07:22.162 --> 00:07:23.489
preserving and trying to get back,

00:07:23.489 --> 00:07:24.661
if there's fathers out there not speaking

00:07:24.661 --> 00:07:25.994
to their daughters.

00:07:25.994 --> 00:07:28.167
You know, when I was
growing up it was very tough

00:07:28.167 --> 00:07:30.026
and people would just
disappear from your lives.

00:07:30.026 --> 00:07:30.859
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:07:30.859 --> 00:07:33.694
- And I finally realized that as an adult

00:07:33.694 --> 00:07:35.521
that I couldn't, and didn't wanna do that,

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:36.590
or go down that road anymore

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:40.386
and that this person right here
was the one love of my life

00:07:40.386 --> 00:07:43.180
and the one I would never
let go for anything.

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:44.098
So I would-

00:07:44.098 --> 00:07:44.931
- Oh wow.

00:07:45.936 --> 00:07:47.099
I love that.

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:49.091
So, it's based on your lives.

00:07:49.091 --> 00:07:50.986
So do y'all also have a
lot of comedy with it?

00:07:50.986 --> 00:07:51.819
You have a lot of humor?

00:07:51.819 --> 00:07:53.986
- Oh yeah, no. I have to
explain that to people.

00:07:53.986 --> 00:07:55.231
- Yeah. I love that though.
- Not but it comes

00:07:55.231 --> 00:07:57.921
from a real place, and the
comedy is, you know, release.

00:07:57.921 --> 00:07:59.942
I feel like laughter is such
a release and so, you know,

00:07:59.942 --> 00:08:01.627
there are sometimes where, the only time

00:08:01.627 --> 00:08:03.980
that we could communicate was
having jokes at each other.

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:05.318
- Exactly.
- Yeah.

00:08:05.318 --> 00:08:07.034
Welcome to all my friendships.

00:08:07.034 --> 00:08:08.257
- Yeah, exactly!
- I was like, wait, what?

00:08:08.257 --> 00:08:09.718
- You know, you give
your friends a hard time,

00:08:09.718 --> 00:08:10.611
you give your family a hard time.

00:08:10.611 --> 00:08:12.116
- That's love. That's a love language.

00:08:12.116 --> 00:08:13.804
- Yes, absolutely.
- For some of us.

00:08:13.804 --> 00:08:16.782
So why or how long did you
two go without totally?

00:08:16.782 --> 00:08:18.776
How long was it, that y'all didn't talk?

00:08:18.776 --> 00:08:19.948
- Sometimes it was on and off,

00:08:19.948 --> 00:08:22.271
but I think the longest
was almost three years

00:08:22.271 --> 00:08:24.446
that we didn't speak?

00:08:24.446 --> 00:08:25.291
- It was three years.
- Yeah.

00:08:25.291 --> 00:08:26.473
- It's a little fuzzy for me

00:08:26.473 --> 00:08:27.596
because it's the same three years

00:08:27.596 --> 00:08:29.541
that those drinks, Four Loko, came out.

00:08:29.541 --> 00:08:31.609
(audience laughing)
So...

00:08:31.609 --> 00:08:33.109
- So it could've been three years,

00:08:33.109 --> 00:08:34.030
it could've been been three minutes.

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:34.863
- Could have been four,

00:08:34.863 --> 00:08:35.851
could have been...
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:08:35.851 --> 00:08:37.918
(Kelly laughing)

00:08:37.918 --> 00:08:39.411
- Night time, daytime. Come on.

00:08:39.411 --> 00:08:41.055
It's the same really
attitude you wanna have.

00:08:41.055 --> 00:08:43.847
And I think sometimes the
further you get away from it,

00:08:43.847 --> 00:08:46.574
the more pride, or the more
that someone is stubborn,

00:08:46.574 --> 00:08:48.269
the more time that goes away from it,

00:08:48.269 --> 00:08:50.728
and you know that it's
probably right to make amends.

00:08:50.728 --> 00:08:53.002
But it becomes so difficult
to take that very first step.

00:08:53.002 --> 00:08:54.469
- But it takes two people to do that.

00:08:54.469 --> 00:08:55.302
- That's exactly, that's right.

00:08:55.302 --> 00:08:57.483
- Yeah. That's the catch.

00:08:57.483 --> 00:09:00.414
So how did you mend fences
enough to make the show?

00:09:00.414 --> 00:09:02.589
I guess my question is how
did you go from not talking

00:09:02.589 --> 00:09:04.426
to all of a sudden like,
"Let's do a show together?"

00:09:04.426 --> 00:09:05.342
- Really the pandemic.

00:09:05.342 --> 00:09:07.096
You know my dad is immunocompromised.

00:09:07.096 --> 00:09:10.084
My mom donated a kidney
to my dad 17 years ago

00:09:10.084 --> 00:09:13.084
and so, you know, he had underlying,

00:09:14.023 --> 00:09:15.299
I was like, what is it, underlying?

00:09:15.299 --> 00:09:16.132
- Yeah, like a situation

00:09:16.132 --> 00:09:18.024
to where he shouldn't have
been around people with, yeah.

00:09:18.024 --> 00:09:20.411
- Exactly. So you know, we
kind of really came together

00:09:20.411 --> 00:09:22.215
as a unit, my mom, my dad and I.

00:09:22.215 --> 00:09:25.361
And you know, I started sharing that,

00:09:25.361 --> 00:09:26.194
it was even interesting,

00:09:26.194 --> 00:09:28.199
my family really hasn't
been together in 10 years

00:09:28.199 --> 00:09:30.005
and so I was just kind of watching.

00:09:30.005 --> 00:09:31.503
And they made one TikTok,

00:09:31.503 --> 00:09:34.243
I was just filming them 'cause
I thought it was hilarious.

00:09:34.243 --> 00:09:36.155
'Cause, you know, even though
my parents were divorced,

00:09:36.155 --> 00:09:37.430
they had so much history together.

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:38.263
- Yeah.
- You know,

00:09:38.263 --> 00:09:40.037
my mom was still trying to fix his hair,

00:09:40.037 --> 00:09:42.140
or pop his zits on his nose and, you know?

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:43.689
So I just thought it was hilarious

00:09:43.689 --> 00:09:46.008
and so I just thought, you
know, we're all inside.

00:09:46.008 --> 00:09:47.664
Someone might laugh and find this

00:09:47.664 --> 00:09:49.973
and then I get it and it
has like 15 million views

00:09:49.973 --> 00:09:51.343
in like, a day.

00:09:51.343 --> 00:09:53.827
- I just love that y'all
came together in Covid

00:09:53.827 --> 00:09:55.751
and like a lot of us didn't make it.

00:09:55.751 --> 00:09:56.794
- Yeah.
- In Covid.

00:09:56.794 --> 00:09:57.650
- No.
- Yeah.

00:09:57.650 --> 00:09:59.377
Like a lot of us didn't.
- It provided opportunity

00:09:59.377 --> 00:10:01.905
and I think, how we took it was, you know,

00:10:01.905 --> 00:10:05.939
we can come together and
family was really important,

00:10:05.939 --> 00:10:08.265
you know, taking inventory
of the people around you

00:10:08.265 --> 00:10:10.220
and perspective.
- Fresh perspectives.

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:11.853
- They came over my house for my birthday

00:10:11.853 --> 00:10:13.347
when we had one of
those pandemic birthdays

00:10:13.347 --> 00:10:15.098
where people just wave
as they go by your house.

00:10:15.098 --> 00:10:15.931
And-

00:10:15.931 --> 00:10:18.025
- He actually had a mariachi band outside.

00:10:18.025 --> 00:10:19.444
- Oh I love a mariachi band.
- It was great.

00:10:19.444 --> 00:10:20.650
- They were just the
guys around the house,

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:22.300
(Kelly laughing)

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:24.186
And, you know, immediately, you know,

00:10:24.186 --> 00:10:25.235
they come into my house

00:10:25.235 --> 00:10:27.396
and just started to throw things away

00:10:27.396 --> 00:10:29.618
that they felt didn't belong in the house,

00:10:29.618 --> 00:10:30.695
although it's my house.

00:10:30.695 --> 00:10:32.371
- Okay, you had like,
eight birthday cakes.

00:10:32.371 --> 00:10:33.272
He had sent, like,

00:10:33.272 --> 00:10:34.270
eight birthday cakes.
- You don't eat them all.

00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:35.606
Yeah.
- But one was made by a woman

00:10:35.606 --> 00:10:37.350
and the other seven were made in a store.

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:39.038
So the one with the woman
had to go in the trash.

00:10:39.038 --> 00:10:40.741
- And then my mom threw it in the trash,

00:10:40.741 --> 00:10:42.834
and then my mom brought
it out of the trash

00:10:42.834 --> 00:10:43.857
and my dad's like, "Take it out."

00:10:43.857 --> 00:10:44.690
And my mom's like, "Okay."

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:45.789
And she put it back, and he's like, "No!"

00:10:45.789 --> 00:10:46.727
(Kelly laughing)

00:10:46.727 --> 00:10:48.104
- And then I took it out of the trash,

00:10:48.104 --> 00:10:49.587
cut a slice and ate it

00:10:49.587 --> 00:10:52.551
because no one's gonna
throw a cake in the trash

00:10:52.551 --> 00:10:53.384
at my house!

00:10:53.384 --> 00:10:55.626
- Yeah. You've at least got to try it.

00:10:55.626 --> 00:10:57.540
- It was tough to wash it
down with the milk I found

00:10:57.540 --> 00:10:59.045
at the bottom of the cake,
but they were like...

00:10:59.045 --> 00:10:59.878
- Dad!
(Kelly laughing)

00:10:59.878 --> 00:11:00.931
- Oh my God, that's gross.

00:11:00.931 --> 00:11:03.001
So wait, your dad famously plays golf?

00:11:03.001 --> 00:11:04.467
- Yes.
- Are you into golf at all?

00:11:04.467 --> 00:11:07.587
- I played in golf just
to spend time with him.

00:11:07.587 --> 00:11:10.461
I actually retired at a very early age

00:11:10.461 --> 00:11:13.421
because there was one
time that he took me out,

00:11:13.421 --> 00:11:14.504
we were up in Pebble Beach,

00:11:14.504 --> 00:11:16.695
and you know, I had my own
little set of clubs and-

00:11:16.695 --> 00:11:18.500
- Your first time to
golf was Pebble Beach?

00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:19.631
- I know.
- Congratulations.

00:11:19.631 --> 00:11:21.764
- Well, thank you.
- I'm like, wow.

00:11:21.764 --> 00:11:24.292
- I know. And so there
was a little course there

00:11:24.292 --> 00:11:25.250
and, you know,

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:27.193
sometimes it's always
been Lopez versus Lopez.

00:11:27.193 --> 00:11:29.983
We kind of sometimes
butt heads and, you know,

00:11:29.983 --> 00:11:30.816
I was getting annoyed.

00:11:30.816 --> 00:11:32.516
He was just trying to
teach me how to swing

00:11:32.516 --> 00:11:33.674
and I was like, "Oh all right!

00:11:33.674 --> 00:11:36.100
You wanna teach me how to swing?
I'll show you how I swing."

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:40.374
And I just put all my might
in it and I got a hole in one.

00:11:40.374 --> 00:11:42.250
- What?
- I got a hole in one.

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:43.083
- You Happy Gilmored the situation?

00:11:43.083 --> 00:11:45.341
- At 11 years old.

00:11:45.341 --> 00:11:47.010
And I just turned around like,

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:48.693
"You know I can't see the ball."

00:11:48.693 --> 00:11:51.809
And my dad's, all the blood
just came out of his face.

00:11:51.809 --> 00:11:52.917
He just was white.

00:11:52.917 --> 00:11:54.407
And then people back there were like,

00:11:54.407 --> 00:11:55.301
"Did you just shoot a hole in one?"

00:11:55.301 --> 00:11:56.426
My dad,

00:11:56.426 --> 00:11:57.259
like, "Yeah."

00:11:57.259 --> 00:11:58.572
- Cause you're-
- You know?

00:11:58.572 --> 00:11:59.920
- Are you both emotions?

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:01.338
You're like angry and proud?

00:12:01.338 --> 00:12:02.350
(Kelly laughing)

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:04.988
- Well no, I mean there's
a second half to the story

00:12:04.988 --> 00:12:06.433
where the ball went in the hole,

00:12:06.433 --> 00:12:10.283
and then Mayan somehow got the
golf cart stuck on the rail.

00:12:10.283 --> 00:12:11.398
So two wheels were moving,

00:12:11.398 --> 00:12:13.108
but none of them touched the ground.

00:12:13.108 --> 00:12:15.589
So, it took a little bit
longer to get back to celebrate

00:12:15.589 --> 00:12:16.422
than we would've liked.

00:12:16.422 --> 00:12:17.255
(Kelly laughing)

00:12:17.255 --> 00:12:18.295
- Because the golf cart got stuck

00:12:18.295 --> 00:12:19.128
on the track.
- She was done.

00:12:19.128 --> 00:12:20.209
She Happy Gilmored it.
- Yeah.

00:12:20.209 --> 00:12:21.210
- I did! I did.

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:22.043
- And she was just missing it

00:12:22.043 --> 00:12:23.395
by that much.
- It's all in the hips.

00:12:23.395 --> 00:12:24.985
You know?
(Kelly laughing)

